00:41:54.250 --> 00:41:57.810
<v Woman>Oh, my god.
<v Woman>哦，天哪。

00:41:57.810 --> 00:42:40.490
<v Isaiah Thompson>We're on a tiny island in the middle of the bay. It's about a mile long, until recently, uninhabited. There are no exits on to the island, just a narrow opening in the guard rails, just before you get to the bridge, and a bumpy dirt path that leads underneath it. Here, hidden from the traffic above, we find a kind of post-apocalyptic outpost, scattered between the giant concrete columns that hold the bridge up are half a dozen wooden shanties, bicycles, and a makeshift fishing peer, a mound of rotting trash. Tents that have been fitted out with lights and furniture are tucked into little concrete alcoves high above us, in the rafters of the bridge, as if a particularly modern group of cavemen lived here.
<v Isaiah Thompson>我们来到了海湾中间的一个小岛上。它大约有一英里长，直到最近还没人居住。岛上没有出口，只有护栏上的一个狭窄开口，就在桥的前面，还有一条颠簸的土路通往桥下。在这里，避开了上方的车流，我们发现了一个类似末日后的前哨站，散落在支撑桥梁的巨大混凝土柱之间的是半打木棚、自行车和一个简易的钓鱼码头，还有一堆腐烂的垃圾。 fitted fittedfitted fitted fitting fitting fitting. 帐篷配备了灯光和家具，塞进我们上方高处的小混凝土凹槽里，在桥的椽子里，仿佛一群特别现代的穴居人住在这里。

00:42:40.490 --> 00:42:46.750
<v Isaiah Thompson>The roar of electric generators blocks out almost every sound but the cars whooshing across the bridge overhead.
<v Isaiah Thompson>发电机的轰鸣声掩盖了除桥上方呼啸而过的汽车以外的几乎所有声音。

00:42:46.750 --> 00:43:17.920
<v Isaiah Thompson>Hello, Patrick. It's Isaiah. I first met Patrick Wiese three years ago, sleeping in a cardboard box. Patrick had just come out of prison, where he'd spent two years and received 10 more years probation for molesting his nine-year-old stepdaughter, a charge to which he pled guilty after turning himself into the police at his wife's request. Considering his circumstances, what was surprising wasn't so much that he had ended up living in a box under a bridge, but how he had come to be right there precisely.
<v Isaiah Thompson>你好，帕特里克。我是以赛亚。我三年前第一次见到帕特里克·维斯时，他正睡在一个纸箱里。帕特里克刚出狱，他在监狱里待了两年，又因为猥亵9岁的继女被判了10年缓刑，他在妻子的要求下向警方自首后对指控认罪。考虑到他的处境，令人惊讶的不是他最终住在桥下的一个盒子里，而是他怎么会精准地在那儿。

