<v Patrick Wiese>I had a woman probation officer at that time, and she told me that I have to live underneath a bridge. I said, wait a minute, you're going to make me homeless? This is crazy. And she let me out the door, told me I have to be there by 10 o'clock at night. So for two or three months I lived, basically, out of a cardboard box.
<v Patrick Wiese>那时我有一个女性缓刑官，她告诉我我必须住在桥下。我说，等一下，你要让我无家可归吗？这太疯狂了。她让我出门，告诉我必须在晚上10点前赶到那儿。所以两三个月里，我基本上就住在一个纸箱里。

00:43:46.030 --> 00:44:30.790
<v Isaiah Thompson>For proof, Patrick extracted a few ratty sheets of paper. One was an official document, a department of corrections letterhead, indicating Patrick's address. Bridge, it said. There are a handful of other places to live 2,500 feet from schools, playground, and daycare centers in the county. They include expensive gated communities most ex-cons can't afford, and the Everglades, where it's illegal to live, and a random apartment building here or there, but they're few and far between. So here's Patrick today. He has a girlfriend and a cell phone, and for a while, a job working at a sub shop making sandwiches.
<v Isaiah Thompson>为了证明，帕特里克拿出几张破旧的纸。其中一份是正式文件，惩教部门的信头，上面写着帕特里克的地址。桥，上面写着。县里距离学校、游乐场和日托中心2500英尺的地方屈指可数。它们包括大多数前罪犯买不起的高档封闭式社区，还有法律规定禁止居住的大沼泽地，以及零星分布的公寓楼，但它们非常少见。这就是现在的帕特里克。他有一个女朋友和一部手机，有一阵子，还在一家三明治店工作。

00:44:30.790 --> 00:45:02.560
<v Patrick Wiese>And I've been here almost three years now. When I first came down here I had nothing. I acquired a tent, and I lived up on underneath the bridge. Now, I lived there for a little over a year in one tent. In that year's time, I accumulated, let's see, a TV, lighting, a generator. As you can see, I have my radio. I have my DVD player. Right here is my shower. It has like a--
<v Patrick Wiese>我已经在这儿快三年了。我刚来这里时什么都没有。我弄了个帐篷，住到桥下。我在那个帐篷里住了一年多。那一年里，我攒下了，你看，一台电视机、照明设备、一台发电机。正如你所看到的，我有我的收音机。我有我的DVD播放机。这就是我的淋浴器。它有一个——

00:45:02.560 --> 00:45:03.430
<v Isaiah Thompson>Wow.
<v Isaiah Thompson>哇。

00:45:03.430 --> 00:45:15.760
<v Patrick Wiese>--a hot bag. What that is a thermal shower. You fill it up with water, you put it in the sun for two hours and it heats up, so you have hot water instead of taking a cold shower. You know, the essentials.
<v Patrick Wiese>——一个热水袋。那是一个热能淋浴器。你把它装满水，放在太阳下晒两个小时它就热了，所以你就有热水洗澡，不用洗冷水澡。你知道，都是必需品。

00:45:15.760 --> 00:45:43.070
<v Isaiah Thompson>What began as three men living here became a kind of colony, a microcivilization. Tonight, it's nearing most people's curfew, 10 o'clock. An occasional car pulls in under the bridge, briefly lighting the place up and then rumbling off to park somewhere by the water. Other than that, it's dark and seedy. An occasional cigarette cherry reveals people hunkered down in the shadows, talking quietly, drinking.
<v Isaiah Thompson>最初只有三个人住在这里，后来变成了一个类似殖民地的微型文明。今晚，快到大多数人的宵禁时间了，晚上10点。偶尔有汽车开进桥下，短暂地照亮这里，然后隆隆地停在水边。除此以外，这里一片漆黑，阴森森的。偶尔闪烁的烟头火光显露出蜷缩在阴影中人们的身影，低声交谈，喝酒。

00:45:43.070 --> 00:45:55.940
<v Isaiah Thompson>Sex offenders getting out of prison are dropped off here like it's the most normal thing in the world. Voncel Johnson ended up under the Julia Tuttle Causeway in 2009. She walks up to us in slippers and a bathrobe.
<v Isaiah Thompson>刚出狱的性犯罪者被送到这里，仿佛这是世界上最正常的事情。冯塞尔·约翰逊2009年最终来到朱莉娅·塔特尔堤道下。她穿着拖鞋和睡袍走近我们。

00:45:55.940 --> 00:46:12.420
<v Voncel Johnson>When I got out of prison, I go-- they told me to go screening report to my probation officer, so I go to her. She tell me, well, I'm sorry, Ms. Johnson, you can't go live where you were supposed to live at. Now I have to go to the Julia Causeway bridge like, what the [BLEEP] is that? I'm thinking, it's OK, it's a treatment. You know, I didn't know.
<v Voncel Johnson>当我出狱时，他们让我去向我的缓刑官报到，所以我去了。她告诉我，好吧，对不起，约翰逊女士，你不能去你原定该住的地方了。现在我必须去朱莉娅堤道桥，这是什么[哔]东西？我想，没关系，这是一种治疗。你知道，我不知道。

00:46:12.420 --> 00:46:18.070
<v Isaiah Thompson>So, in other words, you thought that the bridge was actually just the name of like a halfway house or a treatment.
