<v Voncel Johnson>My probation officer brought me here and told me it's going to be OK. Here it is about 10 o'clock at night, it's pitch dark here, no lights. I see a 1,000 people coming up to my aunt's car like, are you going to be OK? No, I'm not going to be OK. I broke down. I almost had a nervous breakdown. I told her I'd rather go back to prison and do my time and then get out and go where I can live. But they told me I can't do that. I have to sleep under this bridge.
<v Voncel Johnson>我的缓刑官把我带到这里，说会没事的。当时大概是晚上10点，这里一片漆黑，没有灯。我看到1000个人走向我阿姨的车，说，你们会没事的吗？不，我不会没事。我崩溃了。我差点精神崩溃。我告诉她我宁愿回到监狱坐牢，然后再出来去我能住的地方。但他们告诉我我不行。我必须住在这座桥下。

00:46:53.610 --> 00:47:24.560
<v Isaiah Thompson>Like Voncel, a lot of the people living under the bridge have family more than willing to take them in, but whose addresses don't comply with the law. From the start, there were questions about whether the 2,500-foot rule that put all these people under this bridge actually accomplished what it was originally supposed to do, which was protect children. For example, the law only restricts where sex offenders sleep, literally between the hours of 10 at night and 6 in the morning, when no kids are on the street, anyway. They're free to come and go during the day.
<v Isaiah Thompson>像冯塞尔一样，住在桥下的很多人都有非常愿意接收他们的家人，但他们的地址不符合法律规定。从一开始，人们就质疑那个把这些人全部推到这座桥下的2500英尺规定是否真的达到了它原本的目的，也就是保护孩子。例如，法律仅限制性犯罪者睡觉的地点，确切地说是晚上10点到凌晨6点之间，那时根本没有孩子在街上。他们白天可以自由出入。

00:47:24.560 --> 00:47:44.930
<v Isaiah Thompson>Some of them spend the day back at their own houses with their families, including their kids. Jill Levenson is a researcher and social worker at Lynn University in Boca Raton, who specialized in studying sex offender policy, and is as close to a nemesis as Ron Book has in Florida. She says researchers have studied residency restrictions all over the country.
<v Isaiah Thompson>他们中有一些人白天会回到自己的家里陪家人，包括他们的孩子。吉尔·列文森是博卡拉顿林恩大学的研究员和社会工作者，专门研究性犯罪者政策，她是佛罗里达州罗恩·布克的死对头。她说研究人员研究了全国各地的居住限制。

00:47:44.930 --> 00:48:16.180
<v Jill Levenson>From Florida, Minnesota, Colorado, New Mexico, Nevada, New Jersey, New York, Iowa, their conclusions about residence restrictions are remarkably consistent. The results are consistently that there is no relationship between where a sex offender lives and whether or not he reoffends. Sex offenders who live closer to schools or parks or daycare centers do not reabuse children more often than those who live farther away.
<v Jill Levenson>从佛罗里达、明尼苏达、科罗拉多、新墨西哥、内华达、新泽西、纽约、爱荷华，他们关于居住限制的结论非常一致。结果始终是，性犯罪者的居住地点与是否再次犯罪之间没有关联。居住在离学校、公园或日托中心更近的性犯罪者，并不比居住在较远地方的人更常虐待儿童。

00:48:16.180 --> 00:48:31.060
<v Isaiah Thompson>Meanwhile, the practical difficulties of housing the sex offenders was gumming up the works at a number of government agencies, who began pointing fingers at each other and suing. Maria Kayanan from the ACLU represents a number of the former residents from the bridge.
<v Isaiah Thompson>与此同时，安置性犯罪者的实际困难拖慢了许多政府机构的工作，他们开始互相指责并诉讼。美国公民自由联盟（ACLU）的玛丽亚·卡亚南代表了许多从桥上搬走的前住户。

00:48:31.060 --> 00:49:07.880
<v Maria Kayanan>Everybody blames everybody else. The Department of Corrections blames the County for its residency restriction. The County blames the Department of Corrections for having its probation officers tell released offenders that there's no place for them to live in the county other than the Julia Tuttle Causeway. The City of Miami sued the Department of Corrections, the Department of Transportation, and the Department of Motor Vehicle Safety and Licensing-- I may have that backwards-- because they were putting Julia Tuttle Causeway as the official addresses on folks' driver's license and ID cards.
<v Maria Kayanan>每个人都责怪别人。惩教部责怪县政府实行居住限制。县政府责怪惩教部，因为他们的缓刑官告诉被释放的罪犯，除了朱莉娅·塔特尔堤道外，县里没有别的地方可以去。迈阿密市起诉了惩教部、交通部和机动车辆安全与许可部门——我可能记反了——因为他们把朱莉娅·塔特尔堤道作为这些人驾驶执照和身份证件上的官方地址。

00:49:07.880 --> 00:49:17.260
<v Isaiah Thompson>So you're saying the City of Miami sued the DMV for putting Julia Tuttle Causeway on driver's license.
<v Isaiah Thompson>所以你是说迈阿密市起诉机动车管理局在驾照上印朱莉娅·塔特尔堤道地址。

00:49:17.260 --> 00:49:37.530
<v Maria Kayanan>That's right. It's like, if we click our heels together three times and just make their ZIP codes a string of zeroes, the problem will go away.
<v Maria Kayanan>没错。就像如果我们像童话里一样磕三次脚后跟，让他们的邮政编码变成一串零，问题就会消失一样。

00:49:37.530 --> 00:49:57.820
<v Isaiah Thompson>As government agencies struggle with all the repercussions of the rule, officials who are pointing fingers at each other started to point them at Ron Book, the man who got them into this mess and the man with the connections to fix it. And Ron stepped into the fray, happy to lead the charge. Again, Maria Kayanan of the ACLU.
<v Isaiah Thompson>随着政府机构与该规定的所有后果作斗争，那些互相指责的官员开始把矛头指向罗恩·布克，那个把他们带进这个麻烦，并拥有解决它的人脉的人。罗恩也加入了争斗，乐于带头冲锋。还是那位ACLU的玛丽亚·卡亚南。

00:49:57.820 --> 00:50:22.550
<v Maria Kayanan>I just know that he is a force of nature. When he speaks, when he appears, he commands the room. He is omnipresent. No matter whether it's a county commission hearing, whether it's a court hearing, whether it was in our litigation or the city's litigation, [SMACKS THE TABLE] there he was with this Italian suit.
<v Maria Kayanan>我只知道他是一股自然力量。当他说话，当他出现，他统领整个房间。他无处不在。不管是在县委员会听证会，还是在法院听证会，无论是在我们的诉讼还是市政府的诉讼中，[拍打桌子]他穿着那身意大利西装就出现了。

00:50:22.550 --> 00:50:50.210
<v Isaiah Thompson>The most obvious way to fix the problem at the bridge would be for Ron Book to go back to the legislators who originally passed the 2,500-foot rule and roll it back, and that's something Ron is uniquely qualified to make happen. But that's the one thing that's off the table, as far as Ron Book is concerned. He stands by the 2,500-foot rule. In fact, he won't even admit it's the reason people moved under the bridge in the first place, an issue I've been arguing with him going on three years now.
<v Isaiah Thompson>解决桥上问题的最明显方法是让罗恩·布克回去找当初通过2500英尺规定的立法者并撤销它，而罗恩完全有能力做到这一点。但这是罗恩·布克关心的唯一不能谈的话题。他坚持2500英尺的规定。事实上，他甚至不承认这是人们最初搬到桥下的原因，我和他就这个问题争论了三年了。

00:50:50.210 --> 00:51:15.090
<v Ron Book>I don't believe the ordinance made them homeless. I don't believe the ordinance put them under the Julia Tuttle Causeway. I disagree that the ordinance put them there. Their crimes put them there. Their crimes put them their Their unemployability put them there. Their lack of financial resources put them there. I don't truly believe 2,500 feet made them there. And I will not--
<v Ron Book>我不相信这项法令让他们无家可归。我不相信该法令把他们推到了朱莉娅·塔特尔堤道下。我不同意该法令把他们放在那儿。是他们的罪行把他们放在那儿。是他们的罪行。他们的就业能力把他们放在那儿。他们的经济资源缺乏把他们放在那儿。我真的不相信2500英尺让他们呆在那里。而且我不会——

00:51:15.090 --> 00:51:19.980
<v Isaiah Thompson>That's crazy, Ron. There was no one living under the Julia Causeway.
<v Isaiah Thompson>这太疯狂了，罗恩。根本没人住在朱莉娅堤道下。

00:51:19.980 --> 00:51:24.820
<v Ron Book>You're not going to get me to accept that, because I don't, I don't. As a premise, I just don't.
<v Ron Book>你没法让我接受这个观点，因为我不接受。作为一个前提，我就是不接受。

00:51:24.820 --> 00:51:49.390
<v Isaiah Thompson>And so, instead, Book concocted a complicated and fabulously expensive plan, using the resources and staff time at his disposal as the head of the Miami Dade Homeless Trust as well as federal stimulus dollars meant primarily for housing people made homeless by the recession and the foreclosure crisis. His staff began working full-time, trying to move the sex offenders one by one into any legal housing they could find.
<v Isaiah Thompson>所以，布克制定了一个复杂且极其昂贵的计划，利用他作为迈阿密戴德县无家可归者信托基金负责人的资源和员工时间，以及主要用于安置受经济衰退和丧失抵押品赎回权危机影响而无家可归者的联邦刺激资金。他的工作人员开始全职工作，试图把这些性犯罪者一个个安置到他们能找到的任何合法住处。

00:51:49.390 --> 00:52:21.950
<v Ron Book>I will promise you this, that it is the 19 of February-- and I know that I told you this place was going to be closed a little sooner than now-- I assure you that you get to be the 1 of April, the 15 of April, there won't be any more people there. There'll be a piece of guardrail up on the eastbound side and a piece of guardrail up on the westbound side. They're not going to be able drive their cars down there anymore. And those people that continue to have a registration address under the Julia Tuttle Causeway will likely have their doors knocked on and they will be taken back to jail for violating the law.
<v Ron Book>我向你保证，今天是2月19日——我知道我告诉过你这个地方会比现在早一点关闭——我向你保证，到4月1日，4月15日，那儿就不会再有人了。往东的一侧会装上一块护栏，往西的一侧也会装上一块护栏。他们将无法再把车开到那儿去。那些继续把朱莉娅·塔特尔堤道作为登记地址的人，很可能会被敲门，并因为违反法律而被带回监狱。

00:52:21.950 --> 00:52:49.050
<v Isaiah Thompson>On April 1, The Miami Herald reported that the bridge, at last, had been cleared out. All Sex Offenders from Miami's Shanty Town in Housing, said the headline. It was over, except it wasn't over. The day after the bridge had been cleared out, some 30 sex offenders who Book's people had moved into a hotel by the airport were suddenly evicted when one of the men living there was arrested for lewd and lascivious molestation of a minor.
<v Isaiah Thompson>4月1日，《迈阿密先驱报》报道说，桥终于清理干净了。头条标题是“迈阿密贫民窟的所有性犯罪者已安置住房”。一切结束了，除了它并没有结束。桥被清理干净的第二天，布克的人安置在机场附近一家酒店的一些性犯罪者突然被驱逐，因为其中一名住在那里的男子因对未成年人进行猥亵和淫秽骚扰被捕。

00:52:49.050 --> 00:53:17.730
<v Isaiah Thompson>Just two days after the bridge had been cleared out for good, its former residents were sleeping on the floor of their probation offices. Asked if some of the men will be going back under the bridge, Book told reporters, that is not an option. So where were they supposed to go? And where were other sex offenders coming out of prison this year and next year supposed to go? The few pockets of Miami Dade County that had housing that complied with the 2,500-foot rule were already filling up disproportionately with sex offenders.
<v Isaiah Thompson>就在桥被彻底清理干净两天后，其前住户正睡在缓刑办公室的地板上。当被问及这些人中是否有一些会回到桥下时，布克告诉记者，那不是一个选项。那么他们该去哪儿？今年和明年出狱的其他性犯罪者该去哪儿？迈阿密戴德县那几个符合2500英尺规定的住房点，已经不成比例地挤满了性犯罪者。

00:53:17.730 --> 00:54:05.110
<v Isaiah Thompson>Mucking through the sex offender database, I stumbled on a little patch of houses in the middle of miles of farmland, in the middle of nowhere, where, according to the most recent count, 14 sex offenders live alongside families with children. By now, Ron Book has to see all the trouble the 2,500-foot rule is causing. At every turn, he's responded to events by taking on even more responsibility for the lives of these sex offenders. Now he's their landlord, their lawmaker, the arbiter over their fate. But as things have gone wrong, Book has refused to back down or lose face. There have been a few attempts to clean up the current mess by forcing every community in Florida to obey the same statewide residency restriction of 1,000 feet, or maybe 1,500 feet.
<v Isaiah Thompson>在性犯罪者数据库里翻找时，我在荒芜的农田中 stumbled 发现了一小片房屋，根据最新统计，14名性犯罪者与有孩子的家庭住在一起。到目前为止，罗恩·布克一定看到了2500英尺规定造成的麻烦。他在每一个关头都通过承担更多这些性犯罪者生活的责任来回应事件。现在他是他们的房东、立法者、他们命运的裁决者。但随着事情出错，布克拒绝退让或丢脸。有一些试图通过迫使佛罗里达州的每个社区都遵守同样1000英尺，或者也许1500英尺的州居住限制来清理当前烂摊子的尝试。

00:54:05.110 --> 00:54:20.920
<v Isaiah Thompson>Book says that's not good enough, throwing numbers out like he's bargaining for a car. 1,750, he'll sometimes say, might be OK for other communities, not his though. For Book, for Book's community, only 2,500 will do.
<v Isaiah Thompson>布克说那还不够好，抛出数字就像他在讨价还价买车一样。1750，他有时会说，可能对其他社区可以，但他的不行。对于布克，对于布克的社区，只有2500才行。

00:54:20.920 --> 00:54:28.020
<v Ron Book>There's a rational, responsible distance somewhere between 1,750 feet and 2,500 feet.
<v Ron Book>在1750英尺到2500英尺之间一定有一个合理的、负责任的距离。

00:54:28.020 --> 00:54:31.500
<v Isaiah Thompson>What's the difference? It's 250 feet. I mean, what difference does it make?
<v Isaiah Thompson>有什么区别？只有250英尺。我的意思是，那有什么区别？

00:54:31.500 --> 00:55:22.980
<v Ron Book>It's another football field away. And when one looks at 1,000 feet, it's three football fields. Well, I can see three football fields. You're in my 10th floor office having this interview with me. I can see three football fields right now. I can see children three football fields away. I have a powerful set of binoculars I will show you on your way out of my office. I could see five football fields with those binoculars. I don't want a predator and offender, somebody convicted, that has been determined to have committed sexually deviant acts on our children, I do not want that person looking out of their bedroom windows, out of their living room windows, down into a park, down into a playground, that had this person who has a predilection to offend against a child scouting out their next victim.
<v Ron Book>那是另一个足球场的距离。如果你看1000英尺，那是三个足球场。嗯，我能看到三个足球场。你现在正在我10楼的办公室采访我。我现在就能看到三个足球场。我能看到三个足球场外的孩子。我有强力望远镜，待会你离开我办公室时我会展示给你看。用那些望远镜我能看到五个足球场。我不希望一个捕食者和犯罪者，一个被定罪的，被确定曾对我们孩子犯下性行为的人，我不希望那个人从他们的卧室窗口，从他们的客厅窗口向外看，看到一个公园，看到一个游乐场，让这个有犯罪倾向的人去寻找他们的下一个受害者。

00:55:22.980 --> 00:55:35.240
<v Isaiah Thompson>The irony, of course, is that Book doesn't need his binocular to see sex offenders anymore. Now he has to deal with them every day.
<v Isaiah Thompson>讽刺的是，当然，布克现在不需要望远镜也能看到性犯罪者了。现在他每天都得和他们打交道。

00:55:35.240 --> 00:55:42.090
<v Ira Glass>Isaiah Thompson, he's the former host of The Common Rabble. These days, he's a reporter and columnist at the Philadelphia City Paper .
<v Ira Glass>以赛亚·汤普森，他是《平民暴民》的前主持人。这些天，他是《费城市报》的记者和专栏作家。

